# Gradle wrapper LAUNCHER integrity record (build-assurance audit Q3).
#
# gradle/wrapper/gradle-wrapper.properties pins distributionSha256Sum — but the
# jar that ENFORCES that pin, and the gradlew/gradlew.bat launchers that start
# it, had no integrity record of their own: the only tracked executable code in
# the repo without one, while iosApp/Frameworks/tor.xcframework and the vendored
# Argon2 C sources both have one. This code runs with full developer privileges
# on every Android build, every iOS build (an Xcode build phase invokes
# ./gradlew) and every Xcode Cloud run — including on the machine that holds the
# release keystore (kept outside the repo tree). A trojaned jar could simply skip
# its own distribution-checksum check; a trojaned gradlew never invokes the jar
# at all (same sink, sibling path).
#
# Checked by scripts/verify-gradle-wrapper.sh — wired into .githooks/pre-push and
# the release-gates `source-gates` CI job (which runs BEFORE the unit-tests job
# executes ./gradlew).
#
# Regenerate ONLY after a deliberate `gradle wrapper` upgrade:
#   scripts/verify-gradle-wrapper.sh --update
# (the comment header is preserved) and then RE-ANCHOR the jar line against
# Gradle's published wrapper checksum before committing.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# TRUST ANCHOR — the jar line is NOT trust-on-first-use. 76805e32… is the
# checksum Gradle itself publishes for its wrapper jar; verified 2026-08-01
# against BOTH of
#   https://downloads.gradle.org/distributions/gradle-9.1.0-wrapper.jar.sha256
#   https://downloads.gradle.org/distributions/gradle-9.0.0-wrapper.jar.sha256
# (Gradle reuses one wrapper jar across a release line, so several versions
# publish the same digest). The checked-in jar is therefore an unmodified,
# officially published Gradle wrapper jar — not merely "unchanged since someone
# committed it".
#
# NOTE the jar is from the 9.0/9.1 line while distributionUrl pins
# gradle-9.3.1-bin.zip. That is normal and supported — the wrapper jar is
# forward compatible and is NOT required to match the distribution it downloads
# (Gradle 9.3.1 publishes b3a875dd…). Do not "fix" the mismatch by swapping in
# the 9.3.1 jar without regenerating it via `gradle wrapper` and re-anchoring.
#
# gradlew / gradlew.bat have no upstream-published checksum, so those two lines
# are repo-pinned (tamper-evident from this record forward), not
# upstream-anchored. Paths are relative to the repo root.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
7a9ce74cff467ca1bf60a4fcd9f05185acceda4d0f382434d393e17864262c5d  gradle/wrapper/gradle-wrapper.jar
